LOCKS ->
    INTRINSIC & EXPLICIT

    INTRINSIC ->
        These are built into every object in Java.
        When we use synchronized keyword, we use these automatic locks.

    EXPLICIT ->
        These are more advanced locks that we can control using the Lock class in the java.util.concurrent.locks
        You can explicitly say when to lock and unlock, giving more control over how and when people can write in a notebook.

DEADLOCK PREVENTION USING REENTRANT LOCK
        It sets a count of how many locks are in used and how many are unlocked and does not cause deadlock.
        EX: Having a Master key of the house allowing to go anywhere.
        Like enter the house then enter a room and exit the room later exit the house.


        .lock()

        .tryLock()

        .unlock()

        .lockInterruptibly()

DISADVANTAGE OF Synchronized LOCK
1. No guarantee of Fairness
2. Indefinite Blocking
3. Interrupt ability
4. Cannot differentiate between Read/Write locks